use the single-precision tolerance in the single-precision complex qr test,
matching the sibling test directly above; with Intel MKL the two code paths
(qr(a) vs qr(a,b)) differ by ~6e-8, which exceeds sqrt(eps) but is well
within single precision. Still present in Octave 10.2.0.
author: Simon Pinches (ITER Organization)
--- octave-9.2.0.orig/libinterp/corefcn/qr.cc	2026-08-24 15:29:29.758073458 +0200
+++ octave-9.2.0/libinterp/corefcn/qr.cc	2026-08-24 15:29:29.761073513 +0200
@@ -949,8 +949,8 @@
 %! [q, r] = qr (a);
 %! [c, re] = qr (a, b);
 %!
-%! assert (r, re, sqrt (eps));
-%! assert (q'*b, c, sqrt (eps));
+%! assert (r, re, sqrt (eps ("single")));
+%! assert (q'*b, c, sqrt (eps ("single")));
 
 %!test
 %! a = single ([0, 2, 1; 2, 1, 2; 3, 1, 2]);
